<h2>Merge Sort</h2>
<p>
    Merge Sort, often spelled Mergesort, 
    is a divide-and-conquer algorithm that sorts parts of the array, then combines them.
    <br>
    <br>
    Merge Sort begins by splitting the array into two halves.
    Then, it goes into one of the sub-sections and splits it into two halves as well.
    It continues to do for every sub-section until new sub-sections are either empty or only have one element.
    This is the "divide" portion of the algorithm.
    <br>
    <br>
    When a sub-section has one or no elements, it returns its contents as an array 
    (either an empty array or one containing one element).
    Merge Sort then takes these results for two sub-sections and creates a new array.
    Then, it loops through both of the arrays.
    For each step it places the next element from the left sub-section in the new array if it is 
    equal to or smaller than the next element from the right sub-section. 
    Otherwise, it places the next element from the right sub-section in the new array.
    The next elements in the left and right sub-sections are represented by the left and right red bars in the visualization.
    <br>
    <br>
    At this point, Merge Sort has an array that combines all elements from the left and right sub-sections in order.
    This array is then returned as the result for the parent sub-section of the left and right sub-sections.
    Merge Sort repeats this process for each sub-section until it finishes it for the initial two halves of the array.
    This is the "conquer" portion of the algorithm.
    Now Merge Sort has the original array and the sorted array.
    To finish the sort, it simply sets the original array to point towards the new one, and deletes the old array.
    <br>
    <br>
    Since Merge Sort has to make a new array for each section, 
    the largest of which being the same size as the original array, its Space Complexity is <b>n</b>.
    <br>
    <br>
    This visualization shows the sorted array for each sub-section by overwriting
    the sub-section's two halves with the sorted array.
    This helps visualize each section being sorted, but slows down the visualization itself.
</p>

