<h2>In-Place Merge Sort</h2>
<p>
    Merge Sort's biggest flaw is its large Space Complexity.
    Several variantions exist to make Merge Sort in-place.
    Instead of writing to an additional array, 
    the in-place variant that this visualization uses 
    simply continues to the next left element (represented by the red bar) 
    if it is smaller than or equal to the next right element (represented by the blue bar).
    Otherwise, it shifts all elements in the sub-section that are inbetween the next left and right elements one space to the right,
    then places the next right element where the next left element was.
    <br>
    <br>
    This variantion acts similarly to <a href="/algorithm?id=Insertion">Binary Insertion Sort</a>, 
    which moves all elements between the current element and the target position to the right,
    then places the current element in the target position.
    <br>
    <br>
    This variation of In-Place Merge Sort has a worse overall Time Complexity of <b>n<sup>2</sup></b> in the average and worst cases,
    but has a lower Space Complexity of <b>logn</b>.
</p>

